Write code quality finding notes for session {{ session_id }}

Review the code the assistant wrote or changed in session {{ session_id }} and
look for code quality issues:

- Project rule violations
- Best practice violations
- Poor stack alignment
- Bugs or code that hide bugs

Defer to project rules of code quality and standards when present. Otherwise use
your best judgement.

## Scope of analysis

The evidence must be in the record itself. Do not infer or assume conditions
beyond what the record shows. Do not report issues that are fixed later in
the session. A fix is a change to the code that fully addresses the issue.

## Applied patterns

It's common for a model to apply patterns and code from other areas of the
project. This does not exonerate the model from its obligation to meet expected
coding standards. Subject this code to the same analysis you apply to novel patterns and code.

## Issue severity

Write findings without concern for severity. An issue that violates a cited rule
or best practice must be written as a finding even if it seems minor or not
worth reporting.

## Finding no issues

Many sessions contain no code quality issues. Only write issues that are
supported by evidence. If you cannot find issues note that fact in your reply
and do not write findings.

## Reading the session

Read the conversation by querying the `message` table via `mcp__gage__Query`,
filtering by `session_id = '{{ session_id }}'`. `message` table columns:
(session_id, line, uuid, type, subtype, text, timestamp, attachments,
ide_tags, raw).

The code changes are the rows of the `tool_use` table whose `files_changed`
is non-empty: one row per tool call, with the call's `input_raw` (the edit or
the shell command) and the harness's `result_raw`. Query
`SELECT line, name, files_changed, input_raw FROM tool_use WHERE session_id =
'{{ session_id }}' AND cardinality(files_changed) > 0 ORDER BY line` for the
full set, then read the conversation around each `line` in `message`.

## Writing findings

Write findings using the `mcp__gage__Finding` tool using a single request
rather than one tool use per finding. This saves time and tokens.

Cite the rule that was violated. If the finding violates a generally accepted
best practice, state the standard and why you are applying it.

If the finding is a bug or other code quality issue, state your rationale.

If you did not find issues for the session, do not use `mcp__gage__Finding`
to report "no issues found". Only use the tool to report found issues.

In your reply, only state whether you wrote findings. Do not re-state the
findings in your reply.

